Since I lost my ancient wealth
These are they that have nourished my life
In this grotesque, grey desert of the town:
The leaping up of flame;
The widening of the sky at the corner of the street;
The soft renewais of steam at the funnel's lip,
Rising, coiling, dissolving;
White flowers of the roofs
That in unfolding vanish.

You are born; you are no more mine:
I have let you go forever.
Demoniac or divine,
You shall sail by sea and river;

You shall walk by road and track;
You shall fly through wind and weather;
But nevermore come back,
That our hearts may laugh together.

There were two brothers, John and James,
And when the town went up in flames,
To save the house of James dashed John,
Then turned, and lo! his own was gone.

And when the great World War began,
To volunteer John promptly ran;
And while he learned live bombs to lob,
James stayed at home and - sneaked his job.

John came home with a missing limb;
That didn't seem to worry him;
But oh, it set his brain awhirl
To find that James had - sneaked his girl!

Time passed. John tried his grief to drown;
To-day James owns one-half the town;
His army contracts riches yield;
And John? Well, search the Potter's Field.

O, to be a tulip do I long;
A eagle through the airy sphere to glide;
A cloud that rains rainwater down, headlong;
Or be wolf and shout by the woodside.

I'd like to grow myself tall like a pine
Or be the sun and heat some dirt in Spring;
A milkweed wisp on the blue hyaline;
A throstle in the forests whistleing.

I'd like to get all warmed up like a star
And peer out at the earth from the puze azure
Meandering the heavens dark and far,
Gleaming like a jacinth would or sapphire;

Screech like a owl that's lonesome in the sky,
Or be a birdie, high, his nest to weave;
Ramp in the garden like a flutterby
Or roar in people's ear: thunder at eve!

Wouldn't that be swell! Oh, to be free
To improve my image thus, my role to lighten;
Meandering around through Nature endlessly;
Sometimes to comfort folks, sometimes frighten.

No more, my dear, no more these counsels try;
Oh, give my passions leave to run their race;
Let Fortune lay on me her worst disgrace;
Let folk o'ercharg'd with brain against me cry;
Let clouds bedim my face, break in mine eye;
Let me no steps but of lost labour trace;
Let all the earth with scorn recount my case,
But do not will me from my love to fly.
I do not envy Aristotle's wit,
Nor do aspire to Caesar's bleeding fame;
Nor aught do care though some above me sit;
Nor hope nor wish another course to frame,
But that which once may win thy cruel heart:
Thou art my wit, and thou my virtue art.

How wonderfully apt our Maker's Plan,
Who crafted Matter for the use of Man;
Breath'd Oxygen into th' ambient Air
In measured Quantities that all might share;
Spread out the Oceans to their very Shores
And framed firm Apples on their ample Cores;
Created Night as foil to bustling Day-
Dark Night, when all our Toys are put away-
And bade bright Phoebus, when the Night was done,
At once his bold diurnal Course to run;
Who legislated Gravity's strict Laws,
Decree'd for each Effect its needful Cause;
Maintains the Stars in their Trajectories,
And comforts parsons in their rectories.

